15 0 obj
<< /A 43 0 R /Alt (Progressive denoising of an image sequence shows a series of transformed frames, evolving from noise-heavy to clear with visible fish and coral. Arrows labeled \215Denoising\216 indicate each stage. Below, a series of pink ovals labeled \215Transformer\216 illustrate the underlying process, connected by directional arrows.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 32 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
16 0 obj
<< /A 44 0 R /Alt (Diagram of a neural network with two main sections: \(a\) shows feature map transformation using global pooling, shared convolution, sigmoid, and attention vector operations. \(b\) illustrates attention map derivation with element-wise multiply, sigmoid, and convolution filters applied to feature maps. Inputs lead to outputs via feed-forward neural networks and layer normalization. Legend explains symbols: element-wise sum, multiply, sigmoid, convolution.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 33 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 45 0 R /Alt (Line graph titled \215L_pix\216 showing performance over steps. The y-axis is labeled \215Step\216 and the x-axis ranges from 0 to 800. Multiple lines represent different models: DM-AECB_Large, DM-AECB, DM-Trans, DM-CA_Only_Generative, and DM-AECB-Generative, with losses generally decreasing over time.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 35 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 46 0 R /Alt (Six images labeled AECB-1 to AECB-6 display a school of fish swimming over coral in an underwater scene. The images show varying degrees of detail and color saturation, with AECB-1 being the most muted and AECB-6 the most vivid. Each image exhibits the same composition but differs in visual clarity and color intensity.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 36 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
19 0 obj
<< /A 47 0 R /Alt (A comparative analysis of underwater image enhancement techniques is displayed in a grid format. Each row represents a different underwater scene, including marine life and objects on the seabed, processed by various methods such as Fusion, MILLE, WaterNet, FUnIE, Ucolor, Ushape, the proposed method, and Ground Truth \(GT\). The top row shows the original input images, while subsequent rows illustrate the results from different enhancement techniques, culminating in the Ground Truth images on the far right. Each technique presents variations in color accuracy and detail visibility.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 38 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
20 0 obj
<< /A 48 0 R /Alt (Line graph titled \215Validation PSNR vs Validation Steps\216 with five lines representing different models: DM_AECB_02, DM_AECB_06, DM_Trans, DM_CA_Only_Generative, and DM_AECB_Generative. The x-axis shows validation steps from 0 to 25, and the y-axis shows Validation PSNR from 10 to 30. Lines fluctuate irregularly, indicating variance across models. The legend is positioned inside the graph.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 39 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
21 0 obj
<< /A 49 0 R /Alt (Comparison of underwater images showing different noise reduction and enhancement techniques. Rows represent various aquatic scenes including fish and marine life, while columns represent stages from original input, noisy version, and five processing methods: DM-Trans, DM-AECB-Gen, DM-CA-Gen, DM-AECB, and GT. Each method progressively improves the image quality, reducing noise and enhancing clarity.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 39 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
22 0 obj
<< /A 50 0 R /Alt (Line graph showing GPU power usage as a percentage over time in minutes. It includes five datasets labeled: DM-AECB_Large, DM-AECB, DM-Trans, DM-CA_Only_Generative, and DM-AECB-Generative. Usage stabilizes between 70% and 85% after initial fluctuations.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 40 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
